\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BA & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-5) & 0\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 1 \\\\ -1\\cdot 1+2\\cdot (-5) & -1\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 \\\\ -11 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** Notice that \n\n$$\nAB \\neq BA.\n$$\n\nMatrix multiplication is not **commutative**, meaning that when we switch the order of multiplication, we often get a different result (like we did in this example)." 13194139534075 true] [17592186059480 1100 "Find $AB$, if\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ -3 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139534075 false] [17592186059480 1100 "Find $AB$, if \n$[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ -3 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]$" 13194139534075 true] [17592186059480 1145 "Multiplying each row in the first matrix by each column in the second, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB &= \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ -3 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0 + 0 - 9 & -6 + 0 - 3 & 4 + 0 + 6 \\\\ 0 - 1 + 6 & 9 + 2 + 2 & -6 - 4 - 4 \\\\ 0 + 0 + 3 & -6 + 0 + 1 & 4 + 0 - 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-9 & -9 & 10 \\\\ 5 & 13 & -14 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534075 false] [17592186059480 1145 "Multiplying each row in the first matrix by each column in the second, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ -3 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0+0-9 & -6+0-3 & 4+0+6 \\\\ 0-1+6 & 9+2+2 & -6-4-4 \\\\ 0+0+3 & -6+0+1 & 4+0-2\\end{aligned}] \\\\